According to Section 116 of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023, "grievous hurt" is defined as the following kinds of hurt:

(a) Emasculation;
(b) Permanent privation of the sight of either eye;
(c) Permanent privation of the hearing of either ear;
(d) Privation of any member or joint;
(e) Destruction or permanent impairing of the powers of any member or joint;
(f) Permanent disfiguration of the head or face;
(g) Fracture or dislocation of a bone or tooth;
(h) Any hurt which endangers life or which causes the sufferer to be during the space of fifteen days in severe bodily pain, or unable to follow his ordinary pursuits.

In contrast, Section 117 of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023, defines "voluntarily causing grievous hurt" as causing grievous hurt with the intention or knowledge that the hurt is likely to cause grievous hurt, or causing grievous hurt of one kind with the intention or knowledge that the hurt is likely to cause grievous hurt of another kind.

In other words, all grievous hurt is not necessarily hurt, but not all hurt is necessarily grievous.

According to Section 75 of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023, a man committing any of the following acts shall be guilty of the offence of sexual harassment:

(i) physical contact and advances involving unwelcome and explicit sexual overtures;
(ii) a demand or request for sexual favours;
(iii) showing pornography against the will of a woman;
(iv) making sexually coloured remarks.

The punishment for such an offence is rigorous imprisonment for a term which may extend to three years, or with fine, or with both.

Under Section 75 of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023, a man committing any of the following acts is guilty of the offence of sexual harassment:

(i) physical contact and advances involving unwelcome and explicit sexual overtures;
(ii) a demand or request for sexual favours;
(iii) showing pornography against the will of a woman;
(iv) making sexually coloured remarks.

However, the Act does not specifically mention protection for men against sexual offences. The Act primarily focuses on protecting children from sexual offences and provides specific provisions for punishment for various types of sexual harassment and assault.

Therefore, men are not explicitly protected against sexual offences under the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023. However, they can be held liable for committing sexual harassment or assault under the provisions mentioned above.

If you or someone you know has been a victim of sexual harassment or assault, you can file a complaint with the Internal Committee of the workplace or the Local Committee, as per Section 9 of the Sexual Harassment of Women at Workplace (Prevention, Prohibition and Redressal) Act, 2013. The complaint must be filed within three months of the incident, extendable to six months if the Committee is satisfied there was sufficient cause for delay.

For assistance, you can contact the National Commission for Protection of Child Rights (NCPCR) helpline at 011- 49864100 or visit their website at [www.ncprotectionofchildrights.gov.in](http://www.ncprotectionofchildrights.gov.in).

According to Section 51A of the Constitution of India, the fundamental duties of citizens are:

(a) to abide by the Constitution and respect its ideals and institutions, the National Flag and the National Anthem;
(b) to cherish and follow the noble ideals which inspired our national struggle for freedom;
(c) to uphold and protect the sovereignty, unity and integrity of India;
(d) to defend the country and render national service when called upon to do so;
(e) to promote harmony and the spirit of common brotherhood amongst all the people of India transcending religious, linguistic and regional or sectional diversities;
(f) to renounce practices derogatory to the dignity of women;
(g) to value and preserve the rich heritage of our composite culture;
(h) to protect and improve the natural environment including forests, lakes, rivers and wild life, and to have compassion for living creatures;
(i) to develop the scientific temper, humanism and the spirit of inquiry and reform;
(j) to safeguard public property and to abjure violence;
(j) to strive towards excellence in all spheres of individual and collective activity so that the nation constantly rises to higher levels of endeavour and achievement;
(k) who is a parent or guardian to provide opportunities for education to his child or, as the case may be, ward between the age of six and fourteen years.

These fundamental duties are not enforceable by any court, but they are fundamental in the governance of the country and it is the duty of the State to apply these principles in making laws.
